# Lemma Core

## Status

This document contains the fundamental requirements of the Lemma project.

These requirements are authoritative.

Do not modify, delete, weaken, reinterpret, or replace these requirements unless the user explicitly requests a change to the project requirements.

If an implementation request conflicts with these requirements:

1. Identify the conflict.
2. Explain it briefly.
3. Ask the user before changing a core requirement.

Do not silently change product requirements to make implementation easier.

---

# 1. What Lemma Is

Lemma is a student-run mathematical publication and archive.

It publishes student-created mathematical work including:

* mathematical articles
* investigations
* essays
* problems
* reports
* computer-science-related mathematical work

Lemma should feel like a modern mathematical journal and mathematical library, not a generic blog or social network.

---

# 2. Primary Goals

The website must prioritize:

1. Excellent article discovery
2. Excellent mathematical readability
3. Simple authoring
4. Clear peer-review workflow
5. Elegant visual identity
6. Long-term maintainability
7. Scalability to at least 100 published articles

The website must remain intuitive as the archive grows.

Never design a feature assuming Lemma will only contain a small number of articles.

---

# 3. Reader Experience

Readers must be able to:

* visit the website without an account
* understand what Lemma is quickly
* browse articles
* search articles
* filter articles
* browse topics
* browse authors
* read mathematical content comfortably
* view images and graphs
* read source code
* copy code
* discover related articles

Authentication must never be required simply to read public content.

---

# 4. Author Experience

Authors must eventually be able to:

* authenticate
* create drafts
* edit drafts
* write mathematical content
* insert equations
* insert PNG/JPEG images
* insert PNG/JPEG graphs
* insert syntax-highlighted code
* preview articles
* autosave work
* submit articles
* receive revision requests
* resubmit articles
* view publication status

Authoring should not require the author to understand web development.

---

# 5. Mathematical Content

Mathematics is a first-class part of Lemma.

The platform must support:

* KaTeX
* inline equations
* display equations
* definitions
* theorems
* lemmas
* propositions
* corollaries
* proofs
* examples
* remarks
* exercises

Mathematical notation must be rendered accurately and readably.

---

# 6. Topics

The primary topics are:

* Algebra
* Calculus
* Linear Algebra
* Topology
* Arithmetic
* Statistics & Probability
* Number Theory
* Computer Science
* Numerical Analysis

Do not add new top-level mathematical categories without explicit product/design discussion.

Specific concepts should generally be represented as tags rather than additional top-level categories.

---

# 7. Publication Workflow

The intended article workflow is:

DRAFT
→ SUBMITTED
→ UNDER REVIEW
→ REVISION REQUESTED
→ RESUBMITTED
→ APPROVED
→ PUBLISHED

Authors cannot automatically publish articles.

---

# 8. Visual Identity

Lemma uses a parchment/leather-inspired visual identity.

Light mode:

* Background: #F5EDE0
* Primary text: #2B1509
* Secondary text: #4A2410
* Primary accent: #8C4A1E
* Highlight: #B8894A
* Muted/border: #C9A876

Dark mode must remain within the same warm brown/parchment visual family.

Do not introduce an unrelated blue, purple, or indigo brand palette.

---

# 9. Homepage

The initial homepage viewport must be minimal and atmospheric.

It should primarily communicate:

* Lemma
* the square Lemma logo
* the motto/tagline
* a distinctive mathematical background
* a subtle indication that the user can scroll

The homepage should then transition into:

1. Recently Published
2. Featured
3. Explore Mathematics
4. Browse All Articles

The homepage may use scroll-driven animation, but animation must remain restrained and must never interfere with navigation or readability.

---

# 10. Navigation

The primary navigation is a drawer/sidebar.

It should provide access to:

* Home
* Articles
* Topics
* Authors
* About
* Search
* Author Dashboard
* Appearance

The square Lemma logo should appear in the navigation drawer.

---

# 11. Search and Discovery

Article discovery is a core feature.

The system must support searching and filtering by appropriate metadata including:

* title
* author
* topic
* tags
* format
* year
* peer-review status

Search and filtering must remain usable with 100+ articles.

Do not create a visually impressive but cumbersome archive.

---

# 12. Logo

The supplied logo reference image is the authoritative visual reference:

`/public/brand/lemma-logo-reference.png`

When implementing the logo, preserve its identity.

Prefer a scalable native SVG/HTML/CSS implementation when practical.

Do not redesign or reinterpret the logo without explicit instruction.

---

# 13. Deployment

The website will be deployed using Vercel.

The architecture must remain compatible with Vercel.

Vercel should be treated as the deployment/platform layer rather than assuming it is itself the database, authentication provider, or object-storage system.

---

# 14. Product Philosophy

When choosing between two implementations, prefer the one that makes Lemma:

* easier to understand
* easier to navigate
* easier to write for
* easier to maintain
* easier to scale

Do not add complexity merely because a feature is technically interesting.

The reader and author experience are more important than maximizing the number of features.
